exports[`golden corpus — deterministic pipeline snapshots > callout-heavy.pdf — extraction and assembly are stable 1`] = `
{
  "doc": {
    "content": [
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 1,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Study Guide: Acids and Bases",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "This sheet collects the asides you should be able to recall before the assessment. Each box highlights a different kind of point.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "DEFINITION",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "An acid is a substance that donates a proton, while a base is a substance that accepts one. The strength of an acid reflects how completely it dissociates in water.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "WARNING",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Always add acid to water, never water to acid. The reverse can boil violently and splash concentrated acid out of the container.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "TIP",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Remember the pH scale runs from 0 to 14, with 7 being neutral. Each whole step is a tenfold change in hydrogen-ion concentration.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 1,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "More Asides",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "NOTE",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "A buffer solution resists changes in pH when small amounts of acid or base are added. Buffers are central to keeping blood chemistry stable.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "EXAMPLE",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Hydrochloric acid in the stomach has a pH of roughly 1.5 to 3.5, which is acidic enough to begin breaking down the proteins in food.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "KEY POINT",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Neutralisation between an acid and a base always produces a salt and water. The reaction is exothermic and releases measurable heat.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
    ],
    "type": "doc",
  },
  "groundTruth": {
    "encrypted": false,
    "hasTextLayer": true,
    "pageCount": 2,
    "pages": [
      {
        "lineCount": 12,
        "pageNumber": 1,
        "text": "Study Guide: Acids and Bases
This sheet collects the asides you should be able to recall before the assessment. Each box
highlights a different kind of point.
DEFINITION
An acid is a substance that donates a proton, while a base is a substance that accepts one.
The strength of an acid reflects how completely it dissociates in water.
WARNING
Always add acid to water, never water to acid. The reverse can boil violently and splash
concentrated acid out of the container.
TIP
Remember the pH scale runs from 0 to 14, with 7 being neutral. Each whole step is a tenfold
change in hydrogen-ion concentration.",
      },
      {
        "lineCount": 10,
        "pageNumber": 2,
        "text": "More Asides
NOTE
A buffer solution resists changes in pH when small amounts of acid or base are added.
Buffers are central to keeping blood chemistry stable.
EXAMPLE
Hydrochloric acid in the stomach has a pH of roughly 1.5 to 3.5, which is acidic enough to
begin breaking down the proteins in food.
KEY POINT
Neutralisation between an acid and a base always produces a salt and water. The reaction is
exothermic and releases measurable heat.",
      },
    ],
  },
}
`;

exports[`golden corpus — deterministic pipeline snapshots > clean-text.pdf — extraction and assembly are stable 1`] = `
{
  "doc": {
    "content": [
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 1,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "An Introduction to Cellular Respiration",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Overview",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Cellular respiration is the set of metabolic reactions that cells use to convert the chemical energy held in nutrients into adenosine triphosphate, the molecule that powers most cellular work. The process releases waste products and is, in broad terms, the reverse of photosynthesis. Although the reactions are usually summarised by a single equation, respiration is better understood as three connected stages, each taking place in a different part of the cell and each contributing a share of the total energy yield.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "The Three Stages",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Glycolysis",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Glycolysis takes place in the cytoplasm and splits a six-carbon glucose molecule into two molecules of pyruvate. It produces a small net gain of energy and does not itself require oxygen.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "The Citric Acid Cycle",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "In the mitochondrial matrix the citric acid cycle oxidises the products of glycolysis, releasing carbon dioxide and transferring high-energy electrons to carrier molecules for the final stage.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 1,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Key Terms",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "ATP — the universal energy currency of the cell. Pyruvate — the three-carbon product of glycolysis. Electron transport chain — the membrane proteins that drive the final stage. Aerobic — describing a process that depends on oxygen.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 1,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Summary",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Respiration extracts energy from nutrients in stages so that it can be captured efficiently rather than lost as heat. The following sequence is worth committing to memory.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "content": [
              {
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Glucose is split during glycolysis in the cytoplasm.",
                    "type": "text",
                  },
                ],
                "type": "paragraph",
              },
            ],
            "type": "listItem",
          },
          {
            "content": [
              {
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Pyruvate is oxidised by the citric acid cycle in the matrix.",
                    "type": "text",
                  },
                ],
                "type": "paragraph",
              },
            ],
            "type": "listItem",
          },
          {
            "content": [
              {
                "content": [
                  {
                    "text": "Electron carriers feed the transport chain to make most of the ATP.",
                    "type": "text",
                  },
                ],
                "type": "paragraph",
              },
            ],
            "type": "listItem",
          },
        ],
        "type": "orderedList",
      },
    ],
    "type": "doc",
  },
  "groundTruth": {
    "encrypted": false,
    "hasTextLayer": true,
    "pageCount": 2,
    "pages": [
      {
        "lineCount": 15,
        "pageNumber": 1,
        "text": "An Introduction to Cellular Respiration
Overview
Cellular respiration is the set of metabolic reactions that cells use to convert the chemical energy
held in nutrients into adenosine triphosphate, the molecule that powers most cellular work. The
process releases waste products and is, in broad terms, the reverse of photosynthesis.
Although the reactions are usually summarised by a single equation, respiration is better
understood as three connected stages, each taking place in a different part of the cell and each
contributing a share of the total energy yield.
The Three Stages
Glycolysis
Glycolysis takes place in the cytoplasm and splits a six-carbon glucose molecule into two
molecules of pyruvate. It produces a small net gain of energy and does not itself require oxygen.
The Citric Acid Cycle
In the mitochondrial matrix the citric acid cycle oxidises the products of glycolysis, releasing carbon
dioxide and transferring high-energy electrons to carrier molecules for the final stage.",
      },
      {
        "lineCount": 11,
        "pageNumber": 2,
        "text": "Key Terms
ATP — the universal energy currency of the cell.
Pyruvate — the three-carbon product of glycolysis.
Electron transport chain — the membrane proteins that drive the final stage.
Aerobic — describing a process that depends on oxygen.
Summary
Respiration extracts energy from nutrients in stages so that it can be captured efficiently rather
than lost as heat. The following sequence is worth committing to memory.
1. Glucose is split during glycolysis in the cytoplasm.
2. Pyruvate is oxidised by the citric acid cycle in the matrix.
3. Electron carriers feed the transport chain to make most of the ATP.",
      },
    ],
  },
}
`;

