{
  "doc": {
    "content": [
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 1,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Die Photosynthese im Überblick",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Einführung",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Die Photosynthese ist der Prozess, mit dem grüne Pflanzen Lichtenergie in chemische Energie umwandeln. Dabei entstehen Zucker und Sauerstoff, während Kohlendioxid und Wasser verbraucht werden.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Ablauf",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Man unterscheidet die Lichtreaktion und die Dunkelreaktion. Die Größe der Blätter und ihre Stellung zur Sonne beeinflussen, wie viel Energie aufgenommen werden kann. Bei großer Hitze schließen viele Pflanzen ihre Spaltöffnungen, um den Wasserverlust zu verringern. Zusammenfassend lässt sich sagen: ohne Photosynthese gäbe es weder Nahrung noch atembare Luft. Sie ist die Grundlage fast aller Ökosysteme.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
    ],
    "type": "doc",
  },
  "groundTruth": {
    "encrypted": false,
    "hasTextLayer": true,
    "pageCount": 1,
    "pages": [
      {
        "lineCount": 11,
        "pageNumber": 1,
        "text": "Die Photosynthese im Überblick
Einführung
Die Photosynthese ist der Prozess, mit dem grüne Pflanzen Lichtenergie in chemische Energie
umwandeln. Dabei entstehen Zucker und Sauerstoff, während Kohlendioxid und Wasser
verbraucht werden.
Ablauf
Man unterscheidet die Lichtreaktion und die Dunkelreaktion. Die Größe der Blätter und ihre
Stellung zur Sonne beeinflussen, wie viel Energie aufgenommen werden kann. Bei großer Hitze
schließen viele Pflanzen ihre Spaltöffnungen, um den Wasserverlust zu verringern.
Zusammenfassend lässt sich sagen: ohne Photosynthese gäbe es weder Nahrung noch atembare
Luft. Sie ist die Grundlage fast aller Ökosysteme.",
      },
    ],
  },
}
`;

exports[`golden corpus — deterministic pipeline snapshots > image-heavy.pdf — extraction and assembly are stable 1`] = `
{
  "doc": {
    "content": [
      {
        "attrs": {
          "level": 1,
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Illustrated Notes",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "heading",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Diagrams carry information that prose alone cannot, but a figure on its own is rarely enough. Each illustration in these notes is wrapped in enough surrounding text to explain what the reader is meant to take from it, so the import pipeline has both a real text layer to anchor to and a figure to crop and place inline beside that text.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Figure 1. A schematic block diagram. The block diagram above shows how three components pass data from one stage to the next. Notice that the arrangement is strictly left to right: nothing in the diagram feeds backwards. The waveform plotted below, by contrast, is periodic, and its shape repeats at a fixed interval that can be measured directly off the page.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Figure 2. A plotted periodic waveform.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "A larger illustration can occupy most of a page when the detail genuinely matters. The figure below overlaps two fields so that the region where they interact is visible at a glance. When a figure is this large, the caption and the paragraph that introduces it do most of the work of telling the reader where to look first.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
      {
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Figure 3. Two overlapping fields, drawn to scale. Taken together, the three figures move from the abstract to the concrete. The first is a structural sketch, the second is a measurement, and the third is a spatial map. Study notes that mix figures and prose like this are exactly the case the structured importer is built to handle well.",
            "type": "text",
          },
        ],
        "type": "paragraph",
      },
    ],
    "type": "doc",
  },
  "groundTruth": {
    "encrypted": false,
    "hasTextLayer": true,
    "pageCount": 2,
    "pages": [
      {
        "lineCount": 11,
        "pageNumber": 1,
        "text": "Illustrated Notes
Diagrams carry information that prose alone cannot, but a figure on its own is rarely enough. Each
illustration in these notes is wrapped in enough surrounding text to explain what the reader is
meant to take from it, so the import pipeline has both a real text layer to anchor to and a figure to
crop and place inline beside that text.
Figure 1. A schematic block diagram.
The block diagram above shows how three components pass data from one stage to the next.
Notice that the arrangement is strictly left to right: nothing in the diagram feeds backwards. The
waveform plotted below, by contrast, is periodic, and its shape repeats at a fixed interval that can
be measured directly off the page.
Figure 2. A plotted periodic waveform.",
      },
      {
        "lineCount": 8,
        "pageNumber": 2,
        "text": "A larger illustration can occupy most of a page when the detail genuinely matters. The figure below
overlaps two fields so that the region where they interact is visible at a glance. When a figure is this
large, the caption and the paragraph that introduces it do most of the work of telling the reader
where to look first.
Figure 3. Two overlapping fields, drawn to scale.
Taken together, the three figures move from the abstract to the concrete. The first is a structural
